import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path('/workspace/sites/olympiad-atlas');new=[]
def add(y,n,r,t,tags,s,reason):
 url=f'https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/USAMO-{y}-notes.pdf'
 new.append(dict(id=f'us-usamo-{y}-{n}',country='United States',competition='USAMO',year=y,number=str(n),statement=s,solution=f'[View Evan Chen’s worked solution notes]({url}).',sourceUrl=url,solutionUrl=url,solutionKind='source-link',topic=t,tags=tags.split('|'),difficulty=r,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='Statement restated in original wording. Full mathematical argument individually checked; linked author-maintained notes are not labelled official.'))
add(2026,2,7,'Combinatorics','Induction|Merging games|Strengthening the claim',r'A row contains positive integers, each a power of $2$. In one move, erase two adjacent entries and replace them by a power of $2$ strictly greater than both. Continue until only one entry remains. Can one always arrange that this last number is less than four times the initial sum? Prove your answer.','Ordinary induction loses the strict factor-four bound. A sharper bound gives endpoint entries half the weight of interior entries, allowing a split with both inductive subproblems below half the bound. Discovering the strengthened statement and handling singleton end pieces are substantial steps.')
add(2026,5,7,'Geometry','Miquel point|Spiral similarity|Circumcentres',r'Points $D\in BC$, $E\in CA$, $F\in AB$ in triangle $ABC$ satisfy $\angle AFE=\angle BDF=\angle CED$. Let $O_A,O_B,O_C$ be the circumcentres of $AFE,BDF,CED$. Let $M,N,O$ be the circumcentres of $ABC,DEF,O_AO_BO_C$, respectively. Prove $OM=ON$.','The Miquel point of the three small circles reveals a common spiral similarity taking A,B,C to E,F,D. Their circumcentres satisfy a second compatible similarity. Transferring these relations to the three final centres turns the claim into an isosceles-triangle identity; finding the shared similarity is the main challenge.')
add(2025,2,6.5,'Algebra','Polynomials|Pigeonhole principle|Real roots|Rolle theorem',r'Let integers $n>k\ge1$ and a real polynomial $P$ of degree $n$ satisfy $P(0)\ne0$, with all roots distinct. Suppose every real-coefficient divisor $a_kx^k+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$ of $P$ has $a_0a_1\cdots a_k=0$. Prove that $P$ has a nonreal root.','Assume all roots are real and restrict to k+1 of them. Pigeonhole forces two complementary divisors to have a zero coefficient in the same position; removing their distinct linear factors produces consecutive zero coefficients. Rolle’s theorem, or a sign-change argument, then contradicts distinct real roots.')
add(2025,3,7,'Combinatorics','Geometric graphs|Infinite descent|Planarity|Strategy',r'Alice first chooses distinct points $P,Q$ and a subset $S$ of the plane. Bob then places infinitely many cities, with all mutual distances greater than $1$ and no three cities collinear. Draw a straight road $AB$ exactly when, for every other city $C$, some $R\in S$ makes $PQR$ directly similar to $ABC$ or $BAC$ (so corresponding vertex order is respected and orientation is preserved). Alice wins if the road network connects every pair of cities by a finite route and no roads cross. Otherwise Bob wins. Which player can force a win?','Choosing the exterior of a diameter disk creates a planar geometric graph. Connectivity on an infinite set cannot be justified by a finite minimum-spanning-tree argument: a squared-distance descent, dropping by at least one each time, is needed. Designing the region and proving both properties make this an upper-edge problem.')
add(2024,1,7,'Number theory','Divisors|Factorials|Bertrand postulate|Consecutive integers',r'For which integers $n\ge3$ do the divisors $1=d_1<d_2<\cdots<d_k=n!$ satisfy $d_2-d_1\le d_3-d_2\le\cdots\le d_k-d_{k-1}$?','Find two consecutive large divisors of n! after a missing divisor; their gap of one contradicts nondecreasing gaps. A squared integer and one less supply the consecutive divisors, while Bertrand’s postulate supplies the intervening missing prime. The prerequisite prime-distribution result and small cases raise this to the upper edge.')
add(2024,4,6,'Combinatorics','Circular arrangements|Extremal constructions|Pigeonhole principle',r'Determine all positive integer pairs $(m,n)$ for which there exists a circular necklace of $mn$ red or blue beads such that every division into $m$ blocks of $n$ consecutive beads gives pairwise distinct red-bead counts in the blocks.','Pigeonhole gives the obvious bound, but attaining it for every possible offset requires a staircase arrangement of red and blue blocks. Tracking how the counts change when the cuts move proves the construction; equal padding then handles the remaining cases.')
add(2024,6,7,'Combinatorics','Double counting|Cauchy–Schwarz|Set systems|Sharp inequalities',r'Fix integers $n>2$ and $1\le\ell\le n$. Find the largest real $c$ such that, for every positive integer $k$, every family $A_1,\ldots,A_k\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}$ with $|A_i|\ge\ell$ (repetitions allowed), and all nonnegative reals $x_1,\ldots,x_k$, one has $\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^k\sum_{j=1}^k x_ix_j\frac{|A_i\cap A_j|^2}{|A_i||A_j|}\ge c\left(\sum_{i=1}^k x_i\right)^2$.','Expand each squared intersection count into ordered element pairs, then interchange sums to obtain a sum of squares. Separating equal from distinct element pairs gives two sharp Cauchy–Schwarz bounds. A symmetric family of all fixed-size subsets attains both simultaneously. The double-counting transformation is the difficult insight.')
add(2023,2,6.5,'Algebra','Functional equations|Monotonicity|Bounded error|Growth',r'Find all functions $f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ for which $f(xy+f(x))=xf(y)+2$ for all $x,y>0$.','Making two inputs coincide proves monotonicity. A translation identity then traps the function within a bounded distance of a line. Substitution back into the original equation amplifies any deviation and forces exact linearity; this is a sustained argument rather than a few isolated substitutions.')
add(2023,5,6.5,'Number theory','Arithmetic progressions|Modular arithmetic|Grid constructions',r'For an integer $n\ge3$, arrange $1,2,\ldots,n^2$ in an $n\times n$ table so each row can be reordered as an arithmetic progression. Determine those $n$ for which every such arrangement can be made to have each column reorderable as an arithmetic progression, using only permutations within individual rows.','For prime n, residue classes force a dichotomy that gives an explicit rearrangement. For composite n, construct a small group of rows with a prime-factor step and show one required column must contain two entries from the same row. The counterexample and its endpoint argument supply most of the difficulty.')
data=json.loads((p/'data/americas.json').read_text());ids={x['id'] for x in new};data=[x for x in data if x['id'] not in ids]+new
